\begin_layout Standard
In the programming language 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "INTERCAL"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
INTERCAL is an abbreviation of Compiler Language With No Pronounceable Acronym.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 invented in 1972,
 Don Woods and Jim Lyon deliberately constructed a language that in no way resembled existing languages at the time.
 Instructions start with DO,
 PLEASE or PLEASE DO.
 It has some unusual requirements.
 In the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "original manual"
target "https://web.archive.org/web/20110716084816/http://oops.se/~urban/pit/intercal.ps"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (PostScript) the most spectacular errors are however not well explained.
 These are E079 and E099:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Too Rude (E079 PROGRAMMER IS INSUFFICIENTLY POLITE).
 If under about 1/5 of the statements include the keyword PLEASE,
 the compiler rejects the code for lacking manners.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Too Grovelling (E099 PROGRAMMER IS OVERLY POLITE).
 If over about 1/3 of the statements include PLEASE,
 the compiler halts and rejects the code for being insincere or grovelling too much.
\end_layout

