\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Microsoft and the FAA"
target "https://kotaku.com/microsoft-on-faa-ground-stop-it-wasnt-me-2000756749"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 others were made just to prove a point.
 For example,
 the famous and fabulous 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "brainfuck"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brainfuck"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 was designed by Urban Müller in 1993 as a complete language for which he could write the smallest possible 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "compiler"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/222233193/compilers-then"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for the Amiga 2.0 operating system.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Müller got the compiler size down to 240 bytes!
 This was later cut to a 57-byte interpreter in x-86 assembler.
 You can listen to him describing the proliferation of his frankenchild in this video.
 There is a Minecraft implementation and a 256-core brainf*ck CPU.
 People are weird.
 There’s even a 
