\begin_layout Section*
With POWER comes responsibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We write POlish With Evaluation in Reverse.
 POWER.
 This allows us to use the stack well.
 There is a catch.
 You need to use the whole buffalo.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Conventionally,
 most computer languages deal with functions.
 A function takes zero or more arguments,
 and returns precisely one value.
 The thing is,
 sometimes we need to return several values.
 In a language such as JavaScript,
 we fudge this by returning an array of values,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
You can easily do something similar in Perl.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and then say something like:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
const [dax,
 wug] = Foo(bar);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 you can surely return an array on the stack,
 but verbs are not functions.
 They can put whatever they want on the stack;
 and they take what they need.
 This entails both some common sense and some rules.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rules are pretty simple:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A child of a verb (A verb used inside a ‘parent’ verb) can only see what the parent provides.
 Stuff made within a verb is visible to its children,
 but stuff just there on the stack at entry into the parent verb isn’t.
 There is however a MARK verb that allows the parent to “allocate pocket money to the children”.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A verb can limit what it sees on the stack.
 TAKE limits what the verb sees.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is a CLEAN command to scrub a number of items off the stack.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
You will seldom need to use MARK,
 TAKE or CLEAN.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A verb can return whatever it wants,
 but if there’s stuff left on the stack when a script terminates,
 this is an error!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That last rule may seem very surprising to anyone with programming experience.
 In most computer languages,
 where function xyzzy() returns a value,
 you might say maze = xyzzy();
 but if you simply say xyzzy();
 then the returned value is simply discarded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not so in 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 which is intensely stack aware.
 We “use the whole buffalo”,
 only discarding something explicitly.
 Even this is an error:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% Dogwagger 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

{
\backslash
footnotesize
\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

PEND 1M <my_kaboom>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

SET <my_kaboom> < NAG [.I waited.] [.Buffalo.] >
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above code creates a verb called <my_kaboom> and PEND then runs it after waiting a second (A million microseconds).
 But there’s a problem.
 After you acknowledge the NAG message <I waited>,
 the text <Buffalo> is still on the stack—
and then the script effectively ends.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
At the command line,
 “stuff left over” is not a problem,
 as this is desired.
 ADD 1 2 will produce 3 and not an error.
 A spare <Buffalo> will be gainfully available for use.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This forces a “potlatch” error:
 you have perpetrated wasteful behaviour.
 The <Buffalo> cannot simply be left there unconsumed.
 Omit it,
 or JUNK it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The common sense we referred to above is just that.
 Common sense.
 Almost always,
 “functions” that return one value are the way to go.
 That’s why computer programming relies on functions.
 So use them.
 Mostly.
 Always respect the stack,
 and when you get an error,
 don’t just fix it—
learn why you stuffed up.
\end_layout

